<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>in.heyted.ngrok</string>

  <!-- Carries Meta's webhooks from the public internet to the gateway.

       On 19 Sep 2026, minutes before the app was to be published, the tunnel
       was found dead:

           ngrok-error-code: ERR_NGROK_3200   (endpoint offline)

       The local adapter answered 400 on 127.0.0.1:8090 the whole time, so
       nothing in the gateway's own logs said anything was wrong. Publishing
       into that would have pointed Meta at an address that answers nobody,
       and Meta disables a webhook subscription after repeated delivery
       failures — so the cost is not a quiet outage, it is the subscription.

       ngrok was installed and configured, with no launchd job and nothing
       running. Same shape as the caffeinate in ai.ted.awake.plist: a command
       somebody ran once and then had to remember forever.

       The address is pinned with --url because it is registered in the app's
       webhook settings. A changed host is a silent outage of exactly the kind
       above. --log=stdout puts ngrok's own reasons in ~/.hermes/logs/ngrok.log
       beside the gateway's, because "it is offline" is never the useful part.

       Free-tier ngrok allows one agent session per endpoint. If this job is
       failing with ERR_NGROK_334, something else already holds the endpoint —
       a hand-started `ngrok http 8090` in a Terminal window is the usual
       culprit. Stop that, do not add --pooling-enabled. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin/ngrok</string>
    <string>http</string>
    <string>8090</string>
    <string>--url=https://scarily-babbling-cupping.ngrok-free.dev</string>
    <string>--log=stdout</string>
    <string>--log-format=logfmt</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <!-- Matches the gateway's own job: back off rather than spin when ngrok is
       failing for a reason a restart will not fix, such as a revoked token. -->
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>30</integer>

  <key>LimitLoadToSessionType</key>
  <array>
    <string>Aqua</string>
    <string>Background</string>
  </array>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>/Users/vandana.agarwal</string>
  </dict>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/vandana.agarwal</string>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/vandana.agarwal/.hermes/logs/ngrok.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/vandana.agarwal/.hermes/logs/ngrok.error.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
